# Use cases

Every action the site performs goes through a use case in `exchange.usecase`. Implement one of these:

| Interface | When | Method |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ParameterlessUseCase<R>` | no input, one result | `suspend fun execute(): Result<R, AppError>` |
| `ParameterizedUseCase<P, R>` | input, one result | `suspend fun execute(params: P): Result<R, AppError>` |
| `ActionUseCase` | no input, no result | `suspend fun execute()` |

This server answers one request at a time. Do not add Flow use cases.

Pick `ParameterizedUseCase` when there is input. Pick `ParameterlessUseCase` when there is not. `ActionUseCase` only when there is nothing to return.

Return `Result`. Do not return a nullable, a Boolean, or throw for an expected failure (email taken, rejected, not found).

```kotlin
class RegisterUserUseCase(
    private val users: UserRepository,
    private val passwords: PasswordHasher,
) : ParameterizedUseCase<RegisterUser, User> {
    override suspend fun execute(params: RegisterUser): Result<User, AppError> {
        // validate, then users.insert
    }
}
```

```kotlin
// wrong: the route talks to the table and invents its own result type
fun createUser(input: NewUser): CreateUserResult
```

Construct use cases in `App` and pass them into the routes. Business rules (field limits, sticker cycle, password check) live in the use case. Serbian wording lives in the web layer, mapped from `AppError`.
